		{
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can you transfer Vincennes University credits to other major colleges?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Vincennes University offers seamless credit transfer through Indiana's Core Transfer Library. With the Transfer Single Articulation Pathways (TSAP) system, completing an associate degree at VU allows you to transfer 60 full credits directly into matching bachelor's programs at Indiana University, Purdue, Ball State, and other major four-year public institutions as an automatic junior."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does Vincennes University offer hands-on technical training and paid internships?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Through the VU College of Technology, programs like the Advanced Manufacturing Technician (AMT) and Machining Internship Program (MIP) operate on an 'earn-and-learn' model. Students receive advanced laboratory instruction while working roughly 16 hours a week in highly paid internships with major manufacturing and engineering employers, allowing them to earn up to $20,000 while in school."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What are the admission requirements and acceptance rate for Vincennes University?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Vincennes University features a non-competitive, open-door admission policy with an overall acceptance rate of 100 percent. The primary admissions requirement is a high school diploma, high school equivalent certificate, or GED transcript. Standardized SAT or ACT scores are used for academic placement but are not required to secure general admission."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Vincennes University the cheapest residential college in Indiana?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. When evaluating total direct costs—which combine full-time tuition, mandatory fees, housing, and a full meal plan—Vincennes University officially ranks as Indiana's lowest-cost residential college, with an average overall direct cost of around $14,500 for in-state students."
          }
        },
